I recently learned of another reason that makes `xor` fishy for this purpose and that applies even if keys in a map are unique: If hashes have N bits, then the security properties of hash functions make it likely that N random inputs hash to N N-bit strings that are linearly independent over `F_2` (field with two elements) when viewed as elements in `F_2^N`. As a result, Gaussian elimination provides an efficient algorithm to compute, for every desired target hash, a subset of these inputs such that their xored hashes result in that crafted hash value. 

Under adversarial conditions, this makes it very easy to generate collisions assuming sufficiently large inputs to the hash combiner function. Collisions by chance are still pretty unlikely, but since Bazel generally uses cryptographic hash functions rather than just "good" hash functions, this still feels like something we should fix. Discarding local action caches doesn't sound that bad when we do it in a major release, which likely discards them anyway (e.g. due to Bzlmod repo name changes).

@haxorz @justinhorvitz What do you think?
